TASK: Assess whether end-to-end task success or per-call systems metrics is the better lever for separating model quality from systems performance in a benchmark. Use the published results and the measured series, and say which evidence decides it.
ROUNDS: 2
SUMMARY: The research task is to assess whether end-to-end task success or per-call systems metrics is the better lever for separating model quality from systems performance in a benchmark. The web search provided a query related to the relationship between end-to-end task success and per-call systems metrics in the context of a benchmark with published results and measured series. The paper search yielded findings on the same topic, focusing on end-to-end task success, per-call systems metrics, and their role in benchmarking model quality separation with published results and measured series. No fact-checking was performed. Both sources suggest a need to evaluate the evidence from published results and measured series to decide which metric is more effective.
REPORT: ResearchReport(title='Evaluation of End-to-End Task Success vs. Per-Call Systems Metrics for Benchmarking Model Quality', mainText='The research task is to assess whether end-to-end task success or per-call systems metrics is the better lever for separating model quality from systems performance in a benchmark. The web search and paper search provided relevant findings on the relationship between end-to-end task success and per-call systems metrics in the context of a benchmark with published results and measured series. Both sources suggest a need to evaluate the evidence from published results and measured series to decide which metric is more effective.', tldr=['The research aims to determine which metric, end-to-end task success or per-call systems metrics, is more effective for separating model quality from systems performance in a benchmark. Further evaluation of published results and measured series is needed to make a decision.'])
